ID: B3-Ch02a-Intro
TEXT:

 

"Melindra," Daren says once we are well beneath the damp pine canopy, the scent of crushed needles sharp under our boots. "Now is the time to lay out what you know about Ollendor's defenses. We need everything."

Down in the basin, the stone bowl of the city spreads beneath a haze of smoke and static. Cracked seredium spires rise like jagged teeth, their crowns humming with purple lightning, while dark hovering skiffs drift between the upper rings.

"Splendid," Melindra says, tossing a pine cone between her hands. "Though in all honesty, I don't know much more than you do. I spent very little time in the lower districts, and it has been eight years since my exile. A great deal of steel and stone could have shifted."

"Right, the exile," Hadrik says, crossing his thick arms over his leather vest. "I almost forgot that was your beef with the king. What did you even do to get thrown off the continent? You were born here, weren't you?"

"That is personal," Melindra says, her voice turning cool and flat as a slate counter. "I would prefer to leave it unsaid."

"There is still something you are holding back," Daren says, folding his arms until the articulated steel of his gauntlets grinds faintly. "Petty revenge does not bring someone across an ocean and into an armed capital. Why do you want the king dead?"

"Because he stole from me," Melindra says, her tone turning razor-sharp. "Something irreplaceable, and I intend to take it back. That part is none of your concern. Keep your end of the oath and help me reach him. The rest I handle alone."

"As long as you keep your word," Daren says after a measured pause, "we will stay out of your private affairs."

"What about the garrison?" Kate steps forward, pulling her green cloak tight against the biting mountain draft. Post-ritual exhaustion still shadows the hollows of her eyes, but her voice is clipped and sharp. "Anything useful from your time here? A flaw in the golem plating, a blind spot in the patrol routes?"

"The technology is recent," Melindra says. "A few decades ago, the king brought in a rogue lessathi. That collaborator turned the ancient ruins beneath the city into working machinery, and he engineered those iron golems patrolling the perimeter. That is why I had you look upon the walls before we drew up a plan. Breaking through the front gate is suicide. We stay quiet, we get inside without drawing the guard, and we keep our heads down until the king is dead. After that, we fight our way out through whatever metal monstrosities they throw at us."

choice("Continue", B3-Ch02a-Intro2, v_current_scene = B3-Ch02a-Intro2, v_checkpoint_rich = 0)


ID: B3-Ch02a-Intro2
TEXT:

 "How do we slip past those constructs if they maintain continuous patrol along the perimeter?" Daren asks, peering through the needles toward the ditch. "Is their vision impaired after dark?"

"There is no need to crawl through the brush," Melindra says. "We walk straight through the front gate. You present yourself as an enchanter seeking trade for your wares, and the rest of us act as your escort. Gatekeepers rarely turn away coin and craftsmanship. My banishment was confined to the king's inner circle, so the sentries will not recognize me."

"You are overlooking one slight complication," Hadrik says, tapping a heavy knuckle against his braided beard. "We killed the earl of Ollendor in Thilias. We survived the arena sentence and Golmyck signed the discharge papers, but a piece of parchment will hardly keep the king from taking our heads for butchering his brother."

"The king cannot execute you for a murder he knows nothing about," Melindra says. "If word of the earl's demise had reached this valley, black banners would drape every parapet and memorial placards would line the village road. The ambassador kept the news to deliver in person, and we cut straight through the monster tracts while his carriage took the high road. We have at least a day before he arrives. Until then, you are simply foreign contestants seeking commerce."

choice(""But will they let a bunch of armed strangers enter their city so easily?"", B3-Ch02a-Bunch, v_current_scene = B3-Ch02a-Bunch)
choice(""What will we do if they don't buy our story?"", B3-Ch02a-Story, v_current_scene = B3-Ch02a-Story)


ID: B3-Ch02a-Bunch
TEXT:

 "But will they let a bunch of armed strangers enter their city so easily?"

"Why wouldn't they?" Melindra says. "To their eyes, you are bounded by the tournament geas. Since they know nothing of the earl's death, they assume you cannot harm a resident anyway. They have no reason to see you as a threat."

"Her logic holds," Daren says, adjusting the shoulder straps of his plate harness. "Melindra knows the ground better than the rest of us. Keep your hands near your steel nonetheless. If the gatekeeper balks, we prepare for the worst. Melindra, any other details before we step out?"

"Tell the sentry you carry an invitation from a local buyer," Melindra says. "It speeds the inspection. If he demands a name, say your patron insists on anonymity. Unless guard orders tightened drastically during my absence, that will clear us through. One more rule: keep your voices down through the settlement. Silence is better. These villagers live under the heel of an erratic monarch; any hint of our true business will send them running to the garrison. We are on hostile ground."

"Understood," Daren says. "I will handle the talking at the barrier. Stay close."

We break cover and make for the packed-dirt thoroughfare leading toward the main archway. Wooden hovels flank both sides of the road, their plots tilled by peasants hunched over rough, pitted iron tools. A few glance up as our boots crunch the gravel, their gazes carrying the same lifeless exhaustion I saw in the children at the Beacon of Hope. They look long past expecting anything good from strangers.

We hold our tongues as Melindra instructed, keeping an even pace until the iron portcullis looms above us. Daren squares his shoulders and steps up to the sentry stationed before the threshold.

choice("Continue", B3-Ch02a-Unimpressed, v_current_scene = B3-Ch02a-Unimpressed)


ID: B3-Ch02a-Story
TEXT:

 "What will we do if they don't buy our story?"

"Merchants are cleared first because outsiders rarely bring goods here," Melindra says. "If the guard doubts Daren's trade, we submit to standard entry inspection. It involves tedious questioning and delays, but nothing insurmountable."

"Her logic holds," Daren says, adjusting the shoulder straps of his plate harness. "Melindra knows the ground better than the rest of us. Keep your hands near your steel nonetheless. If the gatekeeper balks, we prepare for the worst. Melindra, any other details before we step out?"

"Tell the sentry you carry an invitation from a local buyer," Melindra says. "It speeds the inspection. If he demands a name, say your patron insists on anonymity. Unless guard orders tightened drastically during my absence, that will clear us through. One more rule: keep your voices down through the settlement. Silence is better. These villagers live under the heel of an erratic monarch; any hint of our true business will send them running to the garrison. We are on hostile ground."

"Understood," Daren says. "I will handle the talking at the barrier. Stay close."

We break cover and make for the packed-dirt thoroughfare leading toward the main archway. Wooden hovels flank both sides of the road, their plots tilled by peasants hunched over rough, pitted iron tools. A few glance up as our boots crunch the gravel, their gazes carrying the same lifeless exhaustion I saw in the children at the Beacon of Hope. They look long past expecting anything good from strangers.

We hold our tongues as Melindra instructed, keeping an even pace until the iron portcullis looms above us. Daren squares his shoulders and steps up to the sentry stationed before the threshold.

choice("Continue", B3-Ch02a-Unimpressed, v_current_scene = B3-Ch02a-Unimpressed)


ID: B3-Ch02a-Unimpressed
TEXT:

 "Good day to you," Daren calls out, projecting his voice across the ditch. "Are you the officer in charge of entry clearance?"

The sentry slumps in a wooden chair beside the gatehouse, chewing lazily on a fibrous stalk. He measures us from boots to brows with dead, rheumy eyes, barely registering Daren's greeting. Two massive iron golems flank the iron doors behind him, steam ticking from their chest joints, their hollow visors locked onto our formation.

Daren waits with practiced patience as the guard pushes himself up from the chair and ambles forward, spitting green residue into the dry ditch.

"State your business," the man mutters.

"Commerce," Daren says smoothly. "I am an enchanter of considerable renown across the eastern seas. Daren, the healer in armor."

"Never heard of you."

"Varathia remains isolated from foreign trade," Daren replies without missing a beat. "I am meeting a patron within the walls who requested total discretion regarding the transaction. We have an appointment. Will you open the gate?"

The sentry grinds the stem between his molars, his brow furrowed into permanent, leathered creases that look carved by years of foul weather rather than sudden irritation.

"Password," the guard says.

Daren blinks. "Password?"

"Aye. Password. Any invited merchant gets a phrase from their contact. Without it, you sit outside and wait for registry papers. So, what is it?"

#if(v_b3_ch1_password == 1) {
 The phrase clicks in my memory—the script on that river stone in Melindra's collection. 'The password is griffin beak.' Could that old scrap of rock really carry the phrase for Ollendor's gates?

}
choice(""Uh, the guy we were supposed to meet didn't tell us about any passwords."", B3-Ch02a-Resort, v_current_scene = B3-Ch02a-Resort, v_b3_ch2_pass2 = 0)
choice(""The password is 'shut your mouth and open the gate' "", B3-Ch02a-Resort, v_current_scene = B3-Ch02a-Resort, v_b3_ch2_pass2 = 1)
choice(""We forgot the password."", B3-Ch02a-Resort, v_current_scene = B3-Ch02a-Resort, v_b3_ch2_pass2 = 2)
choice(""The password is 'iron falcon' "", B3-Ch02a-Resort, v_current_scene = B3-Ch02a-Resort, v_b3_ch2_pass2 = 3)
choice(""The password is 'griffin beak' "", B3-Ch02a-Resort, v_current_scene = B3-Ch02a-Resort, v_b3_ch2_pass2 = 4) if (v_b3_ch1_password == 1)


ID: B3-Ch02a-Resort
TEXT:

#if(v_b3_ch2_pass2 == 0) {
 "Uh, the guy we were supposed to meet didn't tell us about any passwords."

"Hardly likely," the guard says, shifting the stalk across his teeth. "Anyone living inside knows the rule. If your man left it out, he isn't in a hurry for your goods. You file the entry petition like every other foreigner."

}
#if(v_b3_ch2_pass2 == 1) {
 "The password is 'shut your mouth and open the gate' "

"Your hireling has a sharp tongue," the sentry says to Daren, cutting his eyes toward me with a dull, menacing squint. "The two iron lads behind me have a sharp touch to match. Shall I introduce him? I wager they would reach an understanding in short order."

"Pay my escort no mind," Daren says smoothly, holding out a placating, leather-gloved hand. "He forgets himself. He does not speak on my behalf. Regrettably, we do not possess the required phrase."

"No phrase, no crossing," the sentry says, spitting into the ditch. "Submit a petition and wait your turn."

}
#if(v_b3_ch2_pass2 == 2) {
 "We forgot the password."

"Then pull out your transceiver and ask this patron of yours to repeat it," the guard says.

"We... attempted contact before reaching the ditch, but the channel was dead," Daren says, forcing an apologetic grimace.

"Then you sit in the queue and wait for the ink to dry on an entry petition, like any other stranger."

}
#if(v_b3_ch2_pass2 == 3) {
 "The password is 'iron falcon' "

" 'Iron falcon'?" The sentry stares at me like I crawled out of a swamp. "Are you simple? That cipher was retired over a year ago. Did you genuinely think an outdated phrase would open iron gates?"

"Right... worth checking," I say.

"No current phrase, no entry," the sentry says. "Submit a petition like every other traveler."

}
#if(v_b3_ch2_pass2 == 4) {
 "The password is 'griffin beak' "

"Wrong," the sentry says flatly. "Whether you pulled that out of the air or someone fed you bad coin, it earns you nothing here. Submit an entry petition like every other traveler."

}
 "What does the petition process entail?" Daren asks.

"For now, you wait," the sentry replies, leaning back into his creaking wooden chair. "I will dispatch a runner to the district magistrate before dusk to request clearance forms. You sign when they arrive. If you want, check back tomorrow, though I wouldn't waste the boot leather. Clearance rarely clears the magistrate's desk in under three days."

"Three days?" Daren's voice cracks slightly before he reins it in, plastering on a rigid, diplomat's smile. "I see. Very well. We shall return to our camp and check on the morrow. Thank you for your time."

The sentry grunts, and we turn around and retrace our steps down the gravel road. We maintain strict silence past the cottages, but the moment the pine shadows close around us, Hadrik erupts.

"Three days!" he roars, throwing his thick hands in the air. "The ambassador will march in three times over before a clerk dips a quill for that parchment! We might as well split their iron gates right now! At least we take the initiative!"

"Force is our absolute last resort," Daren says firmly, stepping into the dwarf's path. "You saw the constructs at the gatehouse. That is merely their outer perimeter. Who knows what wards and automata they hold in reserve through the inner rings? We are not finished. We know entry requires a phrase. All we need is a method to uncover it."

"The farmers must possess the phrase," Leila writes, blue characters glowing in the dim shade of the branches. "They require daily access to the markets for supplies."

"Leila is correct," Illuna says, her voice cool and measured. "A bribe should loosen a tongue. In a settlement this destitute, extra coin usually outweighs local decrees."

"Ollendor is not Thilias," Melindra warns, leaning against a pine trunk and shaking her head. "Here, a broken decree costs you your family, or worse. Few peasants will trade their lives for gold."

choice(""What if we tried to get one of them drunk, instead?"", B3-Ch02a-Drunk, v_current_scene = B3-Ch02a-Drunk, v_b3_ch2_infiltration = 0)
choice(""To be honest, I agree with Hadrik. We should just smash through their front gates."", B3-Ch02a-Smash, v_current_scene = B3-Ch02a-Smash, v_b3_ch2_infiltration = 1)
choice(""I could try to eavesdrop on someone from afar." (Requires Hearing 4)", B3-Ch02a-Eavesdrop, v_current_scene = B3-Ch02a-Eavesdrop, v_b3_ch2_infiltration = 2) if (v_hearing == 4 && v_b3_ch1_unlock != 2)


ID: B3-Ch02a-Drunk
TEXT:

 "What if we tried to get one of them drunk, instead?"

"Now that sounds like proper work," Hadrik says, grinning broadly through his black beard. "I wager these peasants have never tasted authentic dwarven vintage. A single bottle from my pack will buy us all the conversation we need."

"Discretion is essential," Daren warns, frowning as he adjusts his baldric. "We cannot corner villagers in dark alleys without drawing eyes. And asking directly in the taproom will have someone reporting us to the gatehouse before the foam settles."

"Leave the craft to me," Hadrik says, clapping my shoulder with enough force to rattle my teeth. "I know how to lure a thirsty man into private quarters. But we cannot march in as a company. A full warband—especially with you clanking in full plate—will frighten them off. Just Barry and me. Ready for a tavern run, Barry?"

"Lead the way," I say.

"Splendid," Hadrik says. "The rest of you hold here. We will signal via transceiver the moment we have the phrase."

"Keep the channel open if trouble starts," Daren adds. "We cannot assist from the tree line if you get boxed in."

"Aye, aye," Hadrik says, already turning on his heel. "We have it in hand."

We step out from the pine canopy onto the rutted settlement lane, scanning the clustered shacks for a tavern sign. Seeing none, Hadrik flags down a middle-aged man sitting on an overturned crate in a dusty yard.

"Ho there, friend!" Hadrik calls out heartily. "Where does a thirsty traveler find the local taphouse? My companion and I are in dire need of a drink."

The man stares at Hadrik with vacant, rheumy eyes for several seconds, then rises slowly from his crate, walks into his cottage, and latches the heavy wooden door without uttering a word.

"Not a conversationalist," Hadrik mutters to me.

He turns toward another villager turning hard dirt with a rusted spade.

"Good man," Hadrik tries again. "Which way to the alehouse?"

The farmer halts his spade, looks Hadrik over from boots to beard, and jerks his chin silently toward a squat timber hall further down the lane before resuming his digging.

"Clear enough," Hadrik says, shaking his head. "Let's find this drink."

Following the direction, we locate the building easily. Though it carries no sign, two patrons stumble out the sagging doorway, swaying on unsteady boots and reeking of sour, fermented mash.

We push past the heavy leather curtain. The rough-hewn counter stands chest-high on Hadrik. The barkeep barely registers the dwarf until Hadrik raps his heavy knuckles on the timber and demands their strongest draught. The man pours a dark, murky liquor into a stained wooden mug with mechanical indifference.

"You call this your strongest?" Hadrik roars, draining the mug in one massive swallow and slamming the timber down. "By the clans, you surface folk have no notion of proper drink. Here—let me show you real craft!"

He unhitches a heavy glass bottle of dark dwarven ale from his pack and thumps it onto the counter.

"See that?" Hadrik tells the barkeep. "Pure dwarven vintage. Best brew you will ever set to your tongue. Go on, take a pull! I have a whole pack of them."

"No outside liquor," the barkeep says flatly, his face completely rigid as stone. "Take your bottle and drink it in the dirt outside."

"Is that so?" Hadrik retakes the bottle, his brow arching with amusement. "Perhaps I will."

choice("Continue", B3-Ch02a-Drunk2, v_current_scene = B3-Ch02a-Drunk2)


ID: B3-Ch02a-Drunk2
TEXT:

 He pulls the cork with a pop, takes a deep swig, and turns to the handful of sullen patrons hunched over their cups in the gloom.

"Anyone tired of sour swill and miserable rules is welcome to join me," Hadrik announces, holding the bottle aloft. "The dwarf buys the round!"

We step back outside into the gravel road, Hadrik taking another slow draught from the neck.

"You think anyone follows?" I ask under my breath.

"Every time," Hadrik says, pulling a second bottle from his pack and handing it to me. "Different towns, same thirsty souls. Give it five seconds."

Right on cue, the leather curtain parts. A gaunt, red-nosed man in stained homespun emerges, scanning the road eagerly. Catching sight of the dark glass bottle in Hadrik's hand, he shambles over, trailing the sour heat of stale mash.

"There we go," Hadrik murmurs with a smirk.

"You... truly offering that dwarven bottle?" the man asks, licking dry, chapped lips. "A sip, maybe?"

"Tell you what," Hadrik says, lowering his flask. "You took our side against that miserable excuse for a barkeep. I will give you a full bottle to yourself."

"A whole bottle?" The man's eyes widen, his fingers trembling slightly. "That is... uncommonly generous."

"Quality deserves appreciation," Hadrik says, producing another bottle and pressing it firmly into the peasant's hands. "Take it. But not on this porch—I have no desire to listen to that barkeep whine about his stoop. Lead us somewhere quiet where we can drink without interruption."

"I know a spot," the drunkard says, already pulling the cork with his teeth and taking a deep, shuddering gulp. "Follow me."

Hadrik flashes me a grin, and we fall in behind him.

Five minutes through weed-choked alleys bring us to a decrepit stone cottage with shattered shutters and a caved-in porch. Dust and thick cobwebs coat the bare floorboards inside, stripped clean of every scrap of furniture.

The man collapses against the wall with his legs splayed, clutching the bottle like a lifeline. Hadrik and I drop onto the rough timber opposite him, crossing our legs.

"Belonged to a family of mages," the man rasps, gesturing vaguely at the blackened, empty hearth. "Empty over a year now. Maybe two. Disappeared between dusk and dawn, the father and all three boys. When the parish asked the garrison, the guards told us to hold our tongues. So we held our tongues. No one comes near it now—looters took everything worth carrying long ago, and the dead don't return. Cheers."

"Cheers," Hadrik says, and we tap our bottles together with a solid clink.

The man takes another greedy swallow and coughs violently, shaking his head. "Gods above... that hits like an anvil. My head is swimming already."

"Dwarven ale on top of cheap wash will do that," Hadrik chuckles. "Pace yourself, friend. Whatever you leave, you take home."

"I can hold my drink!" the man snaps defensively, taking another stubborn swig. "Been haunting taprooms since I was a boy. No dwarven brew puts me down."

"Good lad," Hadrik says, raising his bottle in salute.

choice("Continue", B3-Ch02a-Drunk3, v_current_scene = B3-Ch02a-Drunk3)


ID: B3-Ch02a-Drunk3
TEXT:

 "So," I say, pretending to take a heavy pull while only wetting my lips with the dark foam, "what do you think happened to the mages who lived here? Arrested? How often do folk vanish around the village?"

"Not often out here in the fields," the drunkard mutters, his speech slurring around the consonants as his eyelids droop. "Inside the walls, though... different story. Rumors creep out every season. People walking down an alley and never coming home. Dark times in the capital. Whatever trade brought you here, finish it fast and clear out. Nothing good grows under that palace."

"At this rate, we won't even clear the outer ditch," Hadrik grumbles, leaning his back against the rough wall timber.

"How so?" the man asks, squinting blearily through the gloom.

"We have a buyer waiting inside to trade for enchanted wares," Hadrik lies smoothly. "The fool neglected to provide the gate phrase, and his transceiver has gone silent. The sentry claims we must wait for clearance petitions, but that's just a stall until we pack up and leave. A complete waste of travel."

"Barred at the gate?" The drunkard shakes his head, staring at the dusty floorboards. "A shame. A bloody shame after coming all this way..."

He trails off, swaying slightly as the potent ale works into his veins.

"Stupid rules," he mutters, spitting onto the floor. "Hardly any foreign trade visits as it is, and the crown turns away honest merchants for 'security.' Paranoid madness, that's what it is. The king sees daggers in every shadow, even among his own subjects."

He takes another heavy draught, his face flushed deep crimson. When he looks up, his eyes carry a reckless, stubborn gleam.

"Listen here," the drunkard says, leaning forward and lowering his voice. "I will give you the phrase. You gave me good drink, and I won't see decent folk turned away empty-handed."

"Are you certain?" Hadrik asks, tilting his head. "The garrison won't take kindly to a breach."

"To the pit with the garrison and the king both!" the man snarls, waving a loose hand. "Words never killed anyone. Just... keep my name out of your mouths. I have no wish to rot in a dungeon for helping an honest trade."

"Not a word," Hadrik promises. "We tell them our buyer finally answered the transceiver."

"Right, then," the drunkard rasps, leaning in close. "This week's phrase is 'gargoyle wings.' If they press on prior weeks, last week was 'broken oars.' That clears you past the iron lads."

"Splendid!" Hadrik slaps his knee. "Much obliged, friend! Wait until the others hear we cracked it."

"Go on and meet your buyer," the man says, waving us off lazily as he slumps back against the stone. "Don't fret over me. I know the way back to my cot."

choice(""That's a good idea! Let's go, Hadrik!"", B3-Ch02a-Shrewd, v_current_scene = B3-Ch02a-Shrewd, v_b3_ch2_infiltration = 0)
choice(""We can still wait for you to finish your drink. We're not in THAT much of a hurry."", B3-Ch02a-Shrewd, v_current_scene = B3-Ch02a-Shrewd, v_b3_ch2_infiltration = 3)


ID: B3-Ch02a-Shrewd
TEXT:

#if(v_b3_ch2_infiltration == 0) {
 "That's a good idea! Let's go, Hadrik!"

"Right," Hadrik says, pushing himself up from the dusty floorboards. "I can finish my ale on the road."

"Safe travels, friends," the drunkard mumbles, waving a limp hand from the corner.

We slip out of the abandoned cottage and take the dirt lane back toward the forest where Daren and the rest of the group wait under cover.

"We could have let the man finish his drink in peace," Hadrik grumbles as our boots crunch the gravel.

"And give him time to sober up and start asking who we really are?" I say. "No thanks. We got the phrase. Lingering only invites trouble."

"Fair point," Hadrik concedes. "Let's bring in the others."

He pulls his transceiver, dials Daren's channel, and keys the rune.

"Daren, you on?"

"Here," Daren responds instantly. "Where are you?"

"Main road," Hadrik says. "Step out from the tree line. We have what we came for."

"Already?" Daren's voice carries clear relief. "Stand by, we are coming out."

As the rest of the group emerges one by one through the pines, I push the cork firmly back into my bottle and hand it to Hadrik.

"You shrewd bastard," Hadrik says, testing the full weight of the glass with a wide grin. "You barely wet your tongue. And here I thought you were finally acquiring a taste for proper dwarven brew."

"Another time, Hadrik," I say. "Another time."

We regroup on the gravel and turn toward the city gatehouse, ready to clear the threshold. But before we take a dozen strides, an unfamiliar voice calls to us from the shadows of the woods.

}
#if(v_b3_ch2_infiltration == 3) {
 "We can still wait for you to finish your drink. We're not in THAT much of a hurry."

"Aye, the others can wait a few extra minutes," Hadrik says, settling back down on the timber. "Savor the vintage, friend."

"To good company," the drunkard slurs, tapping his bottle against Hadrik's.

By the time the bottle empties, the man can barely keep his footing. Hadrik and I haul him up, tucking his arms over our shoulders as we guide him out into the lane. Just past the collapsed porch, he suddenly stiffens, his brow furrowing as he struggles through the drunken haze.

"Wait... almost forgot," he whispers hoarsely, hot breath smelling of hops against my ear. "The words alone aren't enough. You have to... wink three times with your left eye while saying it. New rule from the captain... against eavesdroppers."

"Good catch," Hadrik murmurs back. "That would have been an awkward conversation at the barrier."

"They change the watch orders every month," the drunkard grumbles, leaning heavily against our shoulders. "Can't keep track of it all... My shack is just ahead. Leave me by the fence. I can crawl to the bed."

We deposit him gently beside his gate, exchange a brief farewell, and make our way back down the road toward the pines.

Hadrik keys his transceiver to Daren's channel. "Daren, you on?"

"Here," Daren replies. "Where are you?"

"Main road," Hadrik says. "Step out from the tree line. We have what we came for."

"Already?" Daren's voice carries clear relief. "Stand by, we are coming out."

As the rest of the group emerges one by one through the pines, I push the cork firmly back into my bottle and hand it to Hadrik.

"You shrewd bastard," Hadrik says, testing the full weight of the glass with a wide grin. "You barely wet your tongue. And here I thought you were finally acquiring a taste for proper dwarven brew."

"Another time, Hadrik," I say. "Another time."

We regroup on the gravel and turn toward the city gatehouse, ready to clear the threshold. But before we take a dozen strides, an unfamiliar voice calls to us from the shadows of the woods.

}
choice("Continue", B3-Ch02a-Information, v_current_scene = B3-Ch02a-Information)


ID: B3-Ch02a-Smash
TEXT:

 "To be honest, I agree with Hadrik," I say. "We should just smash through their front gates. The dwarf is right about the surprise. If we return to that sentry with a password after fumbling the first exchange, we paint a target on our foreheads. He is just as likely to wave us into an ambush."

"Exactly!" Hadrik says, clapping a broad palm against his thigh. "Barry sees it. Clean and direct."

"Have you forgotten the iron monstrosities walking the perimeter?" Daren asks, shaking his head with exasperation.

"They are metal automata," Hadrik says dismissively. "No matter how thick the plating, enough force shatters the frame. Those flame jets from their jaws are magical construct fire—your shield can turn them aside, and your blade can drink the heat and throw it right back. While you anchor the barrier, Kate, Flower, and Illuna can blast the constructs with ice and water."

"Those hulls likely carry anti-magic enchantments," Daren counters.

"Given time to compress the ice, my lances can puncture reinforced plate," Kate says, her voice flat and steady. "And raw kinetic force from water lances or fireballs still dents and destabilizes heavy frames, anti-magic or not. We do not need an extended siege. Once we cross the threshold, the golems cannot pursue without tearing up their own streets and crushing citizens."

"Then the plan stands," Hadrik says, his eyes gleaming with battle lust. "I break the gate, and we advance beneath Daren's barrier. Daren, you can maintain the ward while marching?"

"At a crawl," Daren grumbles, resting his palm on his pommel. "Any faster and the perimeter folds. And I have not agreed to this madness."

choice("Continue", B3-Ch02a-Smash2, v_current_scene = B3-Ch02a-Smash2)


ID: B3-Ch02a-Smash2
TEXT:

 "One detail," Melindra cuts in, raising a slender finger. "I am not slinging gale blades into a golem melee. The risk of shredding our own line or clipping a city guard is too high. I will cast the flight currents around Barry, but nothing more."

"Fair enough," Hadrik says. "I draw the golems' attention. The tournament geas keeps me from hitting the local guards anyway, so smashing metal keeps me occupied. The rest of you advance while I hold the line."

"Which leaves the perimeter guards to the rest of us," I say. "With Melindra's air currents, I can reach the parapet archers and clear the crossbow line before they pin Daren down."

"You are dragging me into a slaughter regardless of what I say, aren't you?" Daren sighs, running a heavy gauntlet over his bald scalp.

"Ah," Arraka's voice crackles from the amulet, dripping with malicious glee. "How terrible to have your noble council cast aside. Remember yesterday when I suggested Flower lob fireballs over the parapet to burn them out of their hovels like vermin? You ignored me then. Backing my wisdom might have earned my support now. You threw your leverage away, paladin!"

"Kidding aside, we need everyone aligned," Hadrik says, his tone turning serious. "Every neck is on the line. If anyone has reservations, speak them now, because once iron breaks, we cannot turn back. Clear your heads for five minutes, and we settle it."

We take the pause, sitting among the pine roots to let the heat bleed from the debate. Daren eventually relents, extracted under a firm promise: no unnecessary slaughter, and the city guards are neutralized rather than executed, since they are only soldiers following a tyrant's orders.

With the pact struck, we file out of the pines and make for the thoroughfare. But before our boots clear the gravel verge, a harsh, guttural voice calls out from the undergrowth.

choice("Continue", B3-Ch02a-Information, v_current_scene = B3-Ch02a-Information)


ID: B3-Ch02a-Eavesdrop
TEXT:

 "I could try to eavesdrop on someone from afar," I say. "A villager will need market access eventually."

"A sound approach," Daren says, looking toward the distant road through the boughs. "Can your hearing span that distance from the tree line? We cannot have you pacing the cottages aimlessly. A stranger lingering near homes will draw suspicion."

"I stay in the brush," I say. "I can pick up the chatter from behind the verge. I normally filter out background prattle, but with full focus, I can isolate anyone discussing gate entry and trail them."

"Do not overtax your focus," Daren says, touching his forehead scar. "We need your mind sharp for the city."

"Understood," I say. "I will keep to the perimeter."

I work my way to the forest edge, crouching behind a broad pine trunk with full line of sight to the settlement. I draw a steady breath and let my hearing expand, sifting through the layered noise of the village. It takes intense concentration—dozens of voices bleed together across the dirt lanes. Most of it is petty domestic drudgery, spoken in flat, beaten tones.

In one yard, a man mutters a bitter curse against the tax collectors to his wife, then silences himself abruptly, glancing around in terror of the shadows. The rest is mundane routine: rusted tools clinking against stony soil, sour grain boiling in pots, aching joints complained of in hushed whispers.

choice("Continue", B3-Ch02a-Eavesdrop2, v_current_scene = B3-Ch02a-Eavesdrop2)


ID: B3-Ch02a-Eavesdrop2
TEXT:

 Ten minutes of sorting through the drab murmur finally pays off. A voice near the northern plots mentions hauling turnip sacks through the gatehouse before noon.

I ease out of the brush and fall in thirty paces behind the source: a stooped farmer hauling a squeaking, two-wheeled wooden handcart piled with root vegetables. I keep my distance, pausing to inspect fence posts whenever he slows, matching his trudging pace toward the ditch.

At the barrier, the sentry steps from his chair and demands the weekly phrase.

The farmer does not hesitate: 'gargoyle wings.'

The sentry signals. The iron portcullis groans upward on heavy chains, and the two iron golems part to clear the lane. Once the cart rattles through and the iron drops with a dull thud, I turn on my heel, drawing my transceiver and keying Daren's frequency.

"Daren, you reading?"

"Here," Daren answers immediately. "Did you catch it?"

"Clear as day," I say. "Bring the others to the road."

"On our way."

The group emerges from the pines, and we fall into formation on the gravel. But before we take a dozen strides toward the gate, an unfamiliar voice calls to us from the deep brush.

choice("Continue", B3-Ch02a-Information, v_current_scene = B3-Ch02a-Information)


ID: B3-Ch02a-Information
TEXT:

 "You there... humans!" the voice calls in broken, raspy Common. "Do not approach the gatehouse yet. We carry information you require. Crucial details regarding the gate phrase. Step into the brush and hear our trade."

I narrow my focus toward the thicket. Faint, heavily suppressed auras thrum behind the leaves—ragged and tiny, impossible to spot without knowing where to look. Small frames, broken syntax: goblins.

"Arraka," Illuna says sharply, her hand resting on the golden amulet at her hip. "Why was there no warning of goblins trailing us?"

"What a ridiculous grievance," Arraka snorts from the metal. "Shall I report every vole beneath the roots and every thrush in the branches? I watch for real threats. Those creatures possess barely enough aura to light a candle. If I announced every harmless scavenger in the woods, your ears would bleed."

"Were they lying in wait, or did they shadow us from the road?" Illuna asks.

"They have been behind us since dawn," Melindra says casually, flicking a leaf from her sleeve. "I assumed everyone noticed. They carried no hostile intent, so I let them wander."

"How do you know they are not Tyrath's scouts?" Daren whispers, his knuckles whitening on his hilt. "They could have signaled the dragon hours ago!"

"Tyrath's hunting packs track you wherever you walk," Melindra replies in a low murmur. "Scouts make little difference. Spilling blood over three ragged wanderers changes nothing."

"Humans..." the voice rasps again from the ferns. "Do you step under the boughs or not?"

choice("Suggest that we ignore the goblins and head for the city.", B3-Ch02a-Ignore, v_current_scene = B3-Ch02a-Ignore, v_b3_ch2_goblins = 0)
choice("Suggest that we go kill the goblins, just to be safe.", B3-Ch02a-Kill, v_current_scene = B3-Ch02a-Kill, v_b3_ch2_goblins = 1)
choice("Suggest that we go talk to the goblins and see what they want.", B3-Ch02a-Talk, v_current_scene = B3-Ch02a-Talk, v_b3_ch2_goblins = 2)


ID: B3-Ch02a-Ignore
TEXT:

 "Leave the goblins," I say. "There is no point tangling with them. If they intended to alert Tyrath, the dragon already has our coordinates, and they cannot follow us past the gatehouse anyway. We are wasting daylight. Stick to the approach."

"Sensible enough," Hadrik says with a nod, grunting under his breath.

"Then we keep moving," Daren says, adjusting his scabbard against his thigh. "With any fortune, Tyrath's forces will hesitate to strike within city limits."

Daren leads out onto the packed gravel, and we fall in behind him. The goblin calls after us once more from the treeline, but the voice fades into the rustle of the pines.

We retrace our route through the settlement lanes until the heavy gatehouse looms before us once again. The sentry sits in his chair with the same sour expression, squinting at our approach as we halt before the ditch.

"Back already," he grunts, grinding the fibrous stalk. "Did this 'patron' of yours finally supply the phrase?"

choice("Continue", B3-Ch02a-Ruckus, v_current_scene = B3-Ch02a-Ruckus) if (v_b3_ch2_infiltration == 3)
choice("Continue", B3-Ch02a-Insane, v_current_scene = B3-Ch02a-Insane) if (v_b3_ch2_infiltration != 3)


ID: B3-Ch02a-Kill
TEXT:

 "We cut them down," I say in a low murmur. "Better to be certain. If they haven't signaled Tyrath yet, we buy ourselves another few hours before the next pack descends."

"Agreed," Daren whispers, his hand closing over his crossguard. "Keep your eyes sharp. They could be bait for an ambush."

"Ugh," Hadrik mutters, rolling his thick shoulders in disgust. "Let's finish it quickly. I have no stomach for butchering scrawny runts."

"We are coming in," I call out toward the brush. "Hold your ground."

Daren, Hadrik, and I push through the undergrowth, weapons drawn. We close the distance in seconds, catching the first two before they can raise their rusted shortswords. Daren's blade takes the first through the chest with a wet thud; my dagger clears the second's throat before a scream can form. The third screeches in blind panic, scrambling on all fours through the briars. Hadrik lunges in pursuit, but a compressed lance of ice snaps through the branches, punching cleanly through the goblin's skull with a sickening crack. The creature collapses into the leaf litter and lies still.

"Clean strike," Hadrik calls over his shoulder to Kate.

Melindra saunters up behind us, nudging the nearest corpse with the toe of her boot. "I hate to disappoint you, but these three never served Tyrath. The gear is far too decrepit, even for vanguard scouts."

"Why trail us since dawn if they belong to no warband?" Daren asks, wiping a dark streak from his blade on a patch of moss.

Melindra offers a mild shrug. "A mystery for the ages. You killed them before they could explain."

"Could they belong to Fyron?" I ask, glancing back at Illuna.

"Hardly," Illuna says, stepping over a fallen branch. "Fyron's scouts would not stray leagues from their division for no tactical purpose. They were drifters, beholden neither to the dragon nor the goblin general."

"Monsters regardless," Daren says grimly, sheathing his sword with a sharp click. "Whatever their master, they brought nothing good to our heels. Let's reach the gate."

"Right..." Hadrik grunts, his jaw set hard as he looks away from the bloodied leaves.

We clear the timber and take the road back toward the ditch. The sentry watches us from his chair, his jaw grinding on the green stalk as we halt before the threshold.

"Back already," he grunts. "Did this 'patron' of yours finally supply the phrase?"

choice("Continue", B3-Ch02a-Ruckus, v_current_scene = B3-Ch02a-Ruckus) if (v_b3_ch2_infiltration == 3)
choice("Continue", B3-Ch02a-Insane, v_current_scene = B3-Ch02a-Insane) if (v_b3_ch2_infiltration != 3)


ID: B3-Ch02a-Talk
TEXT:

 "We hear them out," I say quietly. "Being goblins doesn't automatically make them Tyrath's vanguard."

"And if they are?" Daren asks, his brow furrowing with hard suspicion.

"Then we cut them down where they stand," I reply. "Scouts wouldn't shout an invitation if they intended to ambush us—they have no numbers and no leverage. They claim to know the entry phrase, and listening costs us nothing."

"Very well," Daren relents, though his hand stays near his pommel. "Keep your weapons ready. Trust nothing."

"We are coming in," I call toward the thicket. "Stay where you are."

We push through the damp ferns into a small clearing under the pines. Three goblins stand huddled near a rotting trunk, clad in cracked leather boiled stiff with grease, their rusted sidearms dangling from fraying rope belts. The eldest steps forward, offering a low, clumsy bow.

"Greetings, travelers," the old goblin rasps in Common. "We know your time is short, so we speak plainly. We observed your difficulty at the gatehouse. We possess information regarding the phrase required for entry, which we will trade for an item in your possession."

"First question," Daren interrupts, towering over them in steel. "Do you answer to Tyrath?"

"The Great Wyrm?" The goblin blinks, shaking his wrinkled green head emphatically. "No. We have no allegiance to dragon-kind."

"Fyron, then?" I ask.

"We travel alone," the goblin says. "Ollendor permits humanoid monsters within its lower districts. We came seeking sanctuary behind the walls."

"Then why shadow our camp since dawn?" Daren demands. "Why not enter directly?"

The three exchange wary glances before the elder gestures toward Melindra.

"We followed because that woman gathered stones along the riverbank," the goblin says. "A contact left our entry phrase inscribed upon a river pebble, and we believe she collected it by mistake. We offer the missing details in exchange for the stone."

choice("Continue", B3-Ch02a-Unfortunate, v_current_scene = B3-Ch02a-Unfortunate) if (v_b3_ch1_password == 0)
choice("Continue", B3-Ch02a-Dialect, v_current_scene = B3-Ch02a-Dialect) if (v_b3_ch1_password == 1)


ID: B3-Ch02a-Unfortunate
TEXT:

 "None of the stones I gathered carried script," Melindra says flatly. "Common or goblin. I cannot read your tongue, but I recognize tool-marks, and my collection contains nothing of the sort."

"May we examine the stones ourselves?" the goblin asks, taking half a step forward with palms open. "A subtle carving might escape an untrained eye."

"Touch my pack," Melindra says with a sweet, radiant smile that never reaches her eyes, "and a gale blade splits you from crown to groin."

The old goblin freezes, swallowing hard. "I see... Could you perhaps display them in your own palm, one by one?"

"No," Melindra says, her smile never wavering.

"That is... regrettable," the goblin sighs, lowering his pointed ears. "Then our exchange cannot proceed. We apologize for the intrusion. We shall wait here in the event you reconsider."

"That is all?" Daren asks, brow arched. "No lunge for the bag?"

"Gods forbid, no!" the goblin says, clutching his chest. "We are not barbarians."

"Right," Daren mutters. "Then we take our leave."

We step back out to the gravel lane and head for the gatehouse.

"A thorough waste of daylight," Hadrik grunts. "Let's hope the gate yields better fortune."

We reach the barrier for the second time. The sentry watches us from his chair with the same sour sneer as before.

"Back already," he grunts. "Did this 'patron' of yours finally supply the phrase?"

choice("Continue", B3-Ch02a-Ruckus, v_current_scene = B3-Ch02a-Ruckus) if (v_b3_ch2_infiltration == 3)
choice("Continue", B3-Ch02a-Insane, v_current_scene = B3-Ch02a-Insane) if (v_b3_ch2_infiltration != 3)


ID: B3-Ch02a-Dialect
TEXT:

 "None of the stones I gathered carried script," Melindra says flatly. "Common or goblin. I cannot read your tongue, but I recognize tool-marks, and my collection contains nothing of the sort."

"May we examine the stones ourselves?" the goblin asks, taking half a step forward with palms open. "A subtle carving might escape an untrained eye."

"Touch my pack," Melindra says with a sweet, radiant smile that never reaches her eyes, "and a gale blade splits you from crown to groin."

The old goblin freezes, swallowing hard. "I see... Could you perhaps display them in your own palm, one by one?"

"No," Melindra says, her smile never wavering.

"Wait a second, Melindra," I say. "You showed Flower a purple river pebble right before we cleared the forest. It had markings on the flat edge."

Melindra looks over, genuinely curious. "Which one?"

"Small, palm-sized, lavender cast. The scratch marks look faint, but they run along the rim."

Melindra slips her pack from one shoulder and rummages through the interior, pulling out a smooth, purplish pebble. "This one?"

She holds it out in her palm.

"That's it," I say. "Those scratches aren't mineral flaws. They are glyphs from an archaic goblin dialect."

"An older script!" Melindra inspects the rock with newfound interest. "That explains why it looked like natural striation. My mistake. If this belongs to you, take it."

She drops the stone into the old goblin's outstretched hand. He holds it close to his bulbous nose, tracing the lines with a claw.

"Ah, yes," he murmurs. "The phrase for this cycle reads 'gargoyle wings.' Thank you. That is what we needed."

choice("Continue", B3-Ch02a-Dialect2, v_current_scene = B3-Ch02a-Dialect2)


ID: B3-Ch02a-Dialect2
TEXT:

 "How do you get 'gargoyle wings' from that?" I ask, frowning. "The literal glyphs translate to 'griffin beak.'"

"You read the ancient dialect?" The goblin blinks up at me in genuine astonishment. "Impressive. Yes, the raw glyphs spell 'griffin beak,' but our contact employs a substitution transposition. Shift the glyph values according to our cipher, and it resolves as 'gargoyle wings.' You understand?"

"A standard cipher," I say. "Was that the information you offered to trade?"

"No," the goblin says, shaking his head. "The crucial detail is that the phrase alone is insufficient. You must wink three times with your left eye while speaking the words. The garrison introduced the gesture recently to foil eavesdroppers. Phrases rotate weekly, but the physical counter-sign remains fixed."

#if(v_b3_ch2_infiltration != 1 && v_b3_ch2_infiltration != 3) {
 "Now that is worth knowing," I say. "Much obliged."

"Pardon me," Melindra chimes in, adopting her most charming grin. "Would you consider trading that purple pebble back? I grew rather fond of the coloration, and I hate leaving a gap in my collection."

"The stone?" The goblin looks at the pebble in his palm. "Keep it. We required only the inscription."

"How generous!" Melindra says, snatching it back and tucking it into her pocket. "My thanks!"

"With our transaction concluded, we should proceed toward the gate," the goblin says. "Best not to approach all at once. Go ahead, and we will follow shortly."

}
#if(v_b3_ch2_infiltration == 1) {
 "A heavy set of precautions for a provincial gatehouse," I say. "Is the garrison always this skittish?"

"The current king suffers from severe paranoia," the goblin says. "He suspects betrayal in every shadow. The iron automata on the ditch confirm the rumors."

"Pardon me," Melindra chimes in, adopting her most charming grin. "Would you consider trading that purple pebble back? I grew rather fond of the coloration, and I hate leaving a gap in my collection."

"The stone?" The goblin looks at the pebble in his palm. "Keep it. We required only the inscription."

"How generous!" Melindra says, snatching it back and tucking it into her pocket. "My thanks!"

"With our transaction concluded, we should proceed toward the gate," the goblin says. "Best not to approach all at once. Go ahead, and we will follow shortly."

}
#if(v_b3_ch2_infiltration == 3) {
 "Good to have our information corroborated," I say. "Saves us an unpleasant surprise."

"Pardon me," Melindra chimes in, adopting her most charming grin. "Would you consider trading that purple pebble back? I grew rather fond of the coloration, and I hate leaving a gap in my collection."

"The stone?" The goblin looks at the pebble in his palm. "Keep it. We required only the inscription."

"How generous!" Melindra says, snatching it back and tucking it into her pocket. "My thanks!"

"With our transaction concluded, we should proceed toward the gate," the goblin says. "Best not to approach all at once. Go ahead, and we will follow shortly."

}
choice(""Sounds good. Let's go!"", B3-Ch02a-Trouble, v_current_scene = B3-Ch02a-Trouble, v_b3_ch2_goblins = 3)
choice(""It's fine, we're not in a hurry. You can go first instead."", B3-Ch02a-Trouble, v_current_scene = B3-Ch02a-Trouble, v_b3_ch2_goblins = 4)


ID: B3-Ch02a-Trouble
TEXT:

#if(v_b3_ch2_goblins == 4 && v_b3_ch2_infiltration != 2) {
 "It's fine, we're not in a hurry. You can go first instead."

"Very well," the elder goblin says, bowing once more. "We take our leave. Safe crossing."

"Sharp thinking," Hadrik whispers once the trio is out of earshot. "Letting them lead keeps us from looking like fools if the cipher is bad."

"There is that," I say, "but they also have room for error. A first-time failure gets you turned away. If we fail a second exchange with that sentry, we earn a dungeon cell."

"We will have our answer shortly," Daren says, watching through the pine branches. "They are nearly at the ditch."

We watch from cover across the clearing. The goblins halt before the chair, exchange a brief murmur with the sentry, and the iron portcullis rises without hesitation. All three shuffle through, and the iron drops.

"The cipher holds," Hadrik says, grinning. "Let's move. We have lingered in the brush long enough."

We retrace our route past the cottages and halt once more before the gatehouse. The sentry sits in his chair, eyeing us with the same dull suspicion.

"Back again," he grunts. "Did your patron finally answer the call?"

"Reached him on the transceiver," I say, winking three times with my left eye. "The phrase is 'gargoyle wings.'"

The sentry's brow shifts slightly. "Pass."

He gestures behind him. The two iron golems step aside with a heavy grind of gears, and the heavy portcullis groans upward on its chains.

"Keep your blades sheathed inside the perimeter," the sentry warns as we step up. "The garrison does not tolerate brawling."

"Understood," I say. "Smooth business only."

"Move along," he mutters.

We cross the threshold, and the iron portcullis slams shut behind us, vibrating through the stone under our boots.

}
#if(v_b3_ch2_goblins == 4 && v_b3_ch2_infiltration == 2) {
 "It's fine, we're not in a hurry. You can go first instead."

"Very well," the elder goblin says, bowing once more. "We take our leave. Safe crossing."

"Sharp thinking," Hadrik whispers once the trio is out of earshot. "Letting them lead keeps us from looking like fools if the cipher is bad."

"The phrase itself is sound—I already heard the farmer speak it," I say. "I wanted proof of the counter-sign. The gesture is what kept us out."

"We will have our answer shortly," Daren says, watching through the pine branches. "They are nearly at the ditch."

We watch from cover across the clearing. The goblins halt before the chair, exchange a brief murmur with the sentry, and the iron portcullis rises without hesitation. All three shuffle through, and the iron drops.

"That settles the counter-sign," Hadrik says, clapping my shoulder. "Come on. We have lingered in the brush long enough."

We retrace our route past the cottages and halt once more before the gatehouse. The sentry sits in his chair, eyeing us with the same dull suspicion.

"Back again," he grunts. "Did your patron finally answer the call?"

"Reached him on the transceiver," I say, winking three times with my left eye. "The phrase is 'gargoyle wings.'"

The sentry's brow shifts slightly. "Pass."

He gestures behind him. The two iron golems step aside with a heavy grind of gears, and the heavy portcullis groans upward on its chains.

"Keep your blades sheathed inside the perimeter," the sentry warns as we step up. "The garrison does not tolerate brawling."

"Understood," I say. "Smooth business only."

"Move along," he mutters.

We cross the threshold, and the iron portcullis slams shut behind us, vibrating through the stone under our boots.

}
#if(v_b3_ch2_goblins == 3 && v_b3_ch2_infiltration != 1) {
 "Sounds good. Let's go!"

"Safe crossing," the elder goblin calls after us as we break through the ferns toward the road.

We retrace our route past the cottages and halt once more before the gatehouse. The sentry sits in his chair, eyeing us with the same dull suspicion.

"Back again," he grunts. "Did your patron finally answer the call?"

}
#if(v_b3_ch2_goblins == 3 && v_b3_ch2_infiltration == 1) {
 "Sounds good. Let's go!"

"Safe crossing," the elder goblin calls after us as we break through the ferns toward the road.

"Just so we are clear," Hadrik murmurs once we are past the tree line, "if these words fail, we return to splitting the iron gates, correct?"

"Without hesitation," I say.

"Splendid," Hadrik grunts.

We retrace our route past the cottages and halt once more before the gatehouse. The sentry sits in his chair, eyeing us with the same dull suspicion.

"Back again," he grunts. "Did your patron finally answer the call?"

}
#if(v_b3_ch2_goblins == 3) {
 "Reached him on the transceiver," I say, winking three times with my left eye. "The phrase is 'gargoyle wings.'"

The sentry's brow shifts slightly. "Pass."

He gestures behind him. The two iron golems step aside with a heavy grind of gears, and the heavy portcullis groans upward on its chains.

"Keep your blades sheathed inside the perimeter," the sentry warns as we step up. "The garrison does not tolerate brawling."

"Understood," I say. "Smooth business only."

"Move along," he mutters.

We cross the threshold, and the iron portcullis slams shut behind us, vibrating through the stone under our boots.

}
choice("Continue", B3-Ch02a-Communicating, v_current_scene = B3-Ch02a-Communicating)


ID: B3-Ch02a-Communicating
TEXT:

 "What comes first?" Kate asks once the dark entry tunnel opens into the broad lower thoroughfare. "Do we scout the perimeter, or push straight for the palace?"

"We will find ample opportunity to gauge their readiness along the way," Daren says, scanning the wide stone avenue as he keeps his hand near his crossguard. "Without knowing the ambassador's arrival time, wandering the alleys is a luxury we cannot afford. Melindra, which route leads to the palace?"

"Through the Perrelion Plaza," Melindra says, glancing up at the tiered stone ramps. "Assuming the district layout remains intact since my departure, it offers the most direct approach. Stay close."

She takes the lead, weaving through the lower market. Citizens scurry past with downcast eyes, clutching rough sacks of grain, while hovering metallic skiffs hum softly overhead along the skyways, their pilots indifferent to the foot traffic below.

"Leila," Daren asks, glancing down at her, "can you reach your father? Is there any way to signal our presence?"

"No," Leila writes, glowing azure letters hanging in the air beside her shoulder before dissolving into faint sparks. "He carried no equipment during our flight from Thilias, so I have no transceiver channel. He promised he would establish contact once I reached Ollendor, but finding us will take time."

"Our arrival will not remain quiet for long," Daren assures her with a faint, encouraging smile. "Once the commotion begins, he will know where to look."

"Let us hope so," Leila writes.

The surrounding architecture grows stranger with every block. Tall, cylindrical towers with curved glass facades stand alongside four-story residential spires built entirely of riveted metal, their dark plates dusted with a shimmering, iridescent coating of seredium powder. No two structures share a blueprint; the entire district looks like an assortment of competing prototypes thrown together without a master plan.

choice("Continue", B3-Ch02b-Weird, v_current_scene = B3-Ch02b-Weird)


ID: B3-Ch02a-Ruckus
TEXT:

 "Reached him on the transceiver," I say, winking three times with my left eye. "The phrase is 'gargoyle wings.'"

The sentry's brow shifts slightly. "Pass."

He gestures behind him. The two iron golems step aside with a heavy grind of gears, and the heavy portcullis groans upward on its chains.

"Keep your blades sheathed inside the perimeter," the sentry warns as we step up. "The garrison does not tolerate brawling."

"Understood," I say. "Smooth business only."

"Move along," he mutters.

We cross the threshold, and the iron portcullis slams shut behind us, vibrating through the stone under our boots.

